Platform: one-socket Xeon 6780E (Sierra Forest, Xeon 6 E-core) server, SNC off:
one NUMA node, two DSA and two IAA (one work queue each on the first pair), no
AMX or AVX-512, RDT allocation.

Sources (kernel paths are Linux v6.18 (torvalds/linux tag v6.18)):
- Xeon 6780E: 144 cores / 144 threads (no SMT), QAT/DLB/DSA/IAA "2 default devices",
  instruction set AVX2, Intel RDT Yes:
  https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/sku/240362/intel-xeon-6780e-processor-108m-cache-2-20-ghz/specifications.html
- SNC off: one node, so CPU 0's L3 list and node0 both hold every CPU
  (arch/x86/kernel/cpu/resctrl/monitor.c snc_get_config(), which lists
  ATOM_CRESTMONT_X in snc_cpu_ids).
- node<N>/cpulist, cpu<N>/topology/physical_package_id, cache/index3/shared_cpu_list,
  PCI numa_node: Documentation/ABI/stable/sysfs-devices-node,
  Documentation/ABI/stable/sysfs-devices-system-cpu, Documentation/ABI/testing/sysfs-bus-pci.
- DSA 0x0b25 / IAA 0x0cfe on idxd: include/linux/pci_ids.h, drivers/dma/idxd/init.c.
  idxd has no Sierra Forest entry of its own, so the devices carry the SPR ids it
  binds; inferred from that table, not captured.
- avx_vnni, rdt_a, cat_l3, cdp_l3, mba: arch/x86/include/asm/cpufeatures.h.

Layout: sysfs paths and relative symlinks follow the driver core's naming, in
exactly the form tests/fixtures/capture.sh writes (the matrix test re-captures each
tree and requires an identical result).

Not from hardware: BDFs, char-device minors, the kernel release string, the brand
string in model name (built from the ARK processor number) and the cpuinfo flags
line (a subset in cpufeatures.h order). QAT is left out: the PCI id Sierra Forest's
QAT enumerates as is not in a primary source read for this fixture, so doctor
reports QAT 0 of the ARK default.
